NAME
       tr - change, squeeze or delete characters

SYNOPSIS
       tr [-c] [-d] [-s] SET1 [SET2]

DESCRIPTION
       tr reads standard input (it never takes a file name: use < FILE) and
       writes it out with characters replaced: each character of SET1 becomes
       the character at the same place in SET2.

       Sets may use ranges (a-z), escapes (\n newline, \t tab) and classes
       such as [:lower:], [:upper:], [:digit:], [:space:] and [:alpha:].

OPTIONS
       -d, --delete
              Delete the characters in SET1.

       -s, --squeeze-repeats
              Replace a run of the same character (from the last set given) by
              one.

       -c, --complement
              Use every character that is NOT in SET1.

EXAMPLES
       Turn lower case into upper case:

           echo server1 | tr a-z A-Z

       Turn colons into newlines to read PATH:

           echo $PATH | tr : '\n'

       Squeeze runs of spaces into one, then cut a field:

           df -h | tr -s ' ' | cut -d ' ' -f 1,5

       Delete carriage returns from a Windows text file:

           tr -d '\r' < notes.txt > notes-unix.txt

SEE ALSO
       sed(1), cut(1), awk(1)
